Diagnostics: the licensing dialog's pixels are correct all the way to X11

Measurement-only TRACEs, applied on top of 0008.  None of them changes
behaviour.  They exist because the licensing dialog (NUIDialog, class
NetUIHWND, 373x148) renders as a solid black rectangle on screen, and this
answers where in the pipeline that happens -- with the answer being: nowhere
in this tree.

  d3d11/texture.c   every CreateTexture2D desc.  The dialog's two swapchain
                    buffers are misc 0 -- not MISC_SHARED_KEYEDMUTEX -- so this
                    window never goes through 0008's shared-surface code at
                    all.  A different mechanism was suspected and ruled out in
                    one line of log.

  d3d11/shared.c    texture dimensions on every AcquireSync/ReleaseSync and on
                    first attach, so a shared surface's participants can be
                    told apart by size in a mixed trace.

  d3d11/view.c      resource dimensions on every CreateRenderTargetView, to
                    find which RTV belongs to the dialog's 373x148 buffer.

  dxgi/swapchain.c  the presenting window and the running present count.  The
                    dialog's swapchain presents exactly once, ever -- compare
                    dozens for the main frame.

  winex11.drv/init.c three things, in order down the present path:

    - altars-redirect: state at the moment client_surface_update_offscreen
      first redirects the window (map_state, size).  IsViewable, correct
      size -- the redirect itself is not late or wrong.
    - altars-srcpixel: an XGetImage read of the *source* window
      (surface->window, the GL-rendered child) at the instant of blit.  White
      -- Office's own D3D11 draw calls (a background clear plus a 56-instance
      glyph batch, seen with +d3d11) really did land there.
    - altars-dstpixel: the same read on the *destination* -- the toplevel's own
      X window -- immediately after NtGdiStretchBlt + XFlush.  Also white.

So the content is correct at every layer this tree owns: the render target,
the source drawable, and the destination drawable, confirmed by reading actual
X11 pixels with no Wine bookkeeping involved.  A plain, Wine-independent Xlib
client (XGetImage on the same window ID) confirms the same thing outside any
Wine code path at all.  And `xwd -root` at that exact screen rectangle, at that
exact moment, reads solid black.

That is where this stops.  It rules out d3d11, dxgi, wined3d and winex11.drv's
own present logic, all the way down to the X11 protocol.  What is left is
whatever composites X windows onto the actual display under this test's
Xwayland (:77) -- outside this tree entirely.  A plain two-line Xlib program
that creates a window, draws into it once and maps it shows up correctly, so
the display server is not broadly broken; something specific to a window that
has been associated with GL/DRI3 rendering and is then presented by a single
plain XCopyArea appears not to reach the compositor.  Repeating the same present
up to nine times, an external XCopyArea with XSync, a resize round-trip, an
unmap/remap cycle and forcing override-redirect were all tried against a live
run and none of them changed the outcome -- see the writeup in
docs/office365-under-wine.md for the numbers.

Apply with `git apply` inside wine-src, on top of 0008, when this question
comes back.

